{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      ConfigureTab Can Reopen for Active Sets\n      ConfigureTab shows active sets and lets you modify them — add images, change patch count, extend scope. AnnotateTab only annotates, never modifies the set structure.\n      \n        ConfigureTab: \"Edit Set\" mode shows current units + lets you add more\n        AnnotateTab: pure annotation, no set management\n        Clear separation: configure = structure, annotate = content\n      \n      \n        Pros\n          Clean separation of concerns\n          ConfigureTab already has the dataset browser and patch settings\n          AnnotateTab stays focused on drawing\n        \n        Cons\n          Context switch: see a problem in Annotate → go back to Configure → return to Annotate\n          ConfigureTab needs an \"edit existing set\" mode","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1775065005633}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Configure Once, Extend From Annotate\n      ConfigureTab creates the initial set. AnnotateTab gets an \"Extend Set\" panel (replaces the current \"Add Patch\" button) that can:\n      \n        Add more patches to the current image\n        Add new images from the dataset\n        Add more random patches across all images\n      \n      All additions go through the same manifest update path. The ConfigureTab becomes read-only for active sets — you extend from where the work happens.\n      \n        Pros\n          Single place to grow the set (AnnotateTab)\n          Context-aware — you're looking at the image when you decide to add more\n          ConfigureTab stays simple (initial setup only)\n        \n        Cons\n          AnnotateTab gets more responsibility\n          Batch additions (e.g. \"add 5 patches to every image\") need UI in AnnotateTab","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1775065025569}
